{"version":18,"type":"full","exportedAt":1790322426392,"world":{"id":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","name":"The Ash Road","description":"A glassmakers’ village under a mountain that burns quietly. Book one.","coverImageId":null,"theme":null,"continuityStaleThreshold":5,"calendar":null,"createdAt":1790320886581,"updatedAt":1790320886581},"mapLayers":[],"locationMarkers":[],"characters":[{"id":"HYHDdbE2nw3i71n9MM4oh","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","name":"Sal'ka","aliases":[],"description":"","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790321345505,"updatedAt":1790321345505},{"id":"UmOiAwhEvgPlkOpCDV-9b","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","name":"Idrisa Coom","aliases":[],"description":"","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790321169884,"updatedAt":1790321169884},{"id":"Z_mcTAzCrHlwPH_NdIDjM","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","name":"Wren Halloway","aliases":["Wren"],"description":"","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":2,"createdAt":1790320998891,"updatedAt":1790321939208},{"id":"f7_Wjl2K7GbZUDNIOOyT8","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","name":"Bell-Anselm","aliases":[],"description":"","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":1,"createdAt":1790321231649,"updatedAt":1790321231649},{"id":"lPcCMk03PXfEZ1WbZ4sa7","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","name":"Yevet du Marr","aliases":[],"description":"","portraitImageId":null,"tags":[],"isAlive":true,"color":null,"birthDate":null,"version":4,"createdAt":1790321455860,"updatedAt":1790322336113}],"items":[],"characterSnapshots":[{"worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","characterId":"Z_mcTAzCrHlwPH_NdIDjM","eventId":"snRZMYe_QJp0_UVg5MKGE","isAlive":true,"currentLocationMarkerId":null,"currentMapLayerId":null,"inventoryItemIds":[],"inventoryNotes":"","statusNotes":"","travelModeId":null,"id":"jh9qlDTR3W3j44G1L6_jL","sortKey":1,"createdAt":1790321006207,"updatedAt":1790321006207,"version":1}],"characterMovements":[],"itemPlacements":[],"locationSnapshots":[],"itemSnapshots":[],"relationships":[],"relationshipSnapshots":[],"timelines":[{"id":"B5oDRKXiDBEBHgzDygHaB","dayOffset":0,"worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","name":"The Ash Road","description":"","color":"#6366f1","createdAt":1790320898953,"version":1}],"chapters":[{"id":"xYRdH6DDcbzx2Wl9Jmr7P","notes":"","wordGoal":null,"worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","timelineId":"B5oDRKXiDBEBHgzDygHaB","number":1,"title":"Chapter 1","synopsis":"","createdAt":1790320898973,"updatedAt":1790320898973,"version":1}],"events":[{"id":"0GURVDYwffZhHY_VCAnKx","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","chapterId":"xYRdH6DDcbzx2Wl9Jmr7P","timelineId":"B5oDRKXiDBEBHgzDygHaB","title":"The Fourth Firing","description":"","locationMarkerId":null,"involvedCharacterIds":["Z_mcTAzCrHlwPH_NdIDjM","f7_Wjl2K7GbZUDNIOOyT8","UmOiAwhEvgPlkOpCDV-9b","lPcCMk03PXfEZ1WbZ4sa7"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":3,"createdAt":1790321786056,"updatedAt":1790321956370,"version":7},{"id":"J392cQxe8km2uHY0b6Ljq","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":[],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","chapterId":"xYRdH6DDcbzx2Wl9Jmr7P","timelineId":"B5oDRKXiDBEBHgzDygHaB","title":"The Officer’s Ledger","description":"","locationMarkerId":null,"involvedCharacterIds":["UmOiAwhEvgPlkOpCDV-9b","Z_mcTAzCrHlwPH_NdIDjM","lPcCMk03PXfEZ1WbZ4sa7"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":1,"createdAt":1790321397208,"updatedAt":1790321493045,"version":5},{"id":"Kyy5yZWCcUl7nAeEEJtdY","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":["f7_Wjl2K7GbZUDNIOOyT8","UmOiAwhEvgPlkOpCDV-9b"],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","chapterId":"xYRdH6DDcbzx2Wl9Jmr7P","timelineId":"B5oDRKXiDBEBHgzDygHaB","title":"What Sal'ka Carried","description":"","locationMarkerId":null,"involvedCharacterIds":["Z_mcTAzCrHlwPH_NdIDjM","HYHDdbE2nw3i71n9MM4oh"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":2,"createdAt":1790321535150,"updatedAt":1790322336963,"version":17},{"id":"snRZMYe_QJp0_UVg5MKGE","travelDays":null,"inWorldTime":null,"tension":null,"structureBeat":null,"status":"draft","povCharacterId":null,"isFlashback":false,"mentionedCharacterIds":["UmOiAwhEvgPlkOpCDV-9b"],"threadIds":[],"motifIds":[],"worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","timelineId":"B5oDRKXiDBEBHgzDygHaB","chapterId":"xYRdH6DDcbzx2Wl9Jmr7P","title":"Third Firing","description":"","locationMarkerId":null,"involvedCharacterIds":["Z_mcTAzCrHlwPH_NdIDjM","f7_Wjl2K7GbZUDNIOOyT8","HYHDdbE2nw3i71n9MM4oh"],"involvedItemIds":[],"tags":[],"sortOrder":0,"createdAt":1790320898992,"updatedAt":1790322338646,"version":15}],"travelModes":[],"timelineRelationships":[],"crossTimelineArtifacts":[],"mapRoutes":[],"mapRegions":[],"mapRegionSnapshots":[],"mapAnnotations":[],"loreCategories":[],"lorePages":[],"factions":[],"factionMemberships":[],"factionRelationships":[],"knowledgeFacts":[],"knowledgeReveals":[],"characterGoals":[],"sceneTexts":[{"id":"46u7s56xUlIuBSnXZS2oe","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"snRZMYe_QJp0_UVg5MKGE","text":"The furnace had been eating for two days and it was still hungry.\n\nWren Halloway crouched at the feeding door with her sleeves pushed past the old burn, watching the glory hole breathe. Orange, then white, then orange again. Master Idrisa Coom said you could read a furnace the way a midwife reads a labour — that the third firing was where a batch came right or came ruinous, and that there was no third thing. Idrisa was asleep upstairs and had been since the bell, which was how Wren knew this firing was hers.\n\nBell-Anselm came down the ladder backwards, as he always did, with the ash bucket knocking each rung.\n\n“It’s white at the back,” he said. “It’s been white at the back an hour.”\n\n“I know what it is.” Wren did not look up. “Put the bucket down and stop breathing on it.”\n\nHe put the bucket down. He was twelve and had the furnace boy’s cough already, the one Idrisa Coom said came out of the ash and not out of the cold, and Wren had stopped arguing with her about it, because arguing with Idrisa Coom was a way of losing an evening.\n\nThey watched it together. Somewhere past midnight the white went out of the back wall and the whole chamber settled to one even colour, the colour of an apricot held up against a lamp, and Wren let out a breath she had been holding since the bell.\n\n“There,” she said.\n\n“Is that it?”\n\n“That is the whole of it. Four years to learn to see that.”\n\nThe door at the yard end opened and the cold came in ahead of the woman carrying it. Sal'ka had ash on her forearms to the elbow and a sack over one shoulder. She set the sack down without being asked and looked into the glory hole the way a farrier looks at a horse she has shod before.\n\n“Third firing,” Sal'ka said. “You’re late with it.”\n\n“We’re on time with it.”\n\n“You’re late,” she said, “because I’ve come up the ash road twice this week, and the second time there was a man on it. Going up. Not coming down.”\n\nBell-Anselm looked at Wren. Wren looked at the furnace, which had nothing to say about any of it.","wordCount":383,"createdAt":1790321148655,"updatedAt":1790322355048},{"id":"7BRtwYLOa1qnYtdYEwmmL","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"0GURVDYwffZhHY_VCAnKx","text":"The fourth firing was set for the night of the new moon, which Idrisa Coom called superstition and observed anyway.\n\nWren banked it herself at dusk. She had the ash ledger open on the slate — Idrisa’s book, not the officer’s — and she read down the delivery column with a taper in her left hand until she found the two entries, and then she read them again, and they were in handwriting she had been taught to copy.\n\nBell-Anselm came in at the ninth hour with the ash and had carried all nine bushels himself, and his hands were shaking in a way that had stopped being about the weight some time before the yard gate.\n\n“Sit down.”\n\n“I am not —”\n\n“Sit down, and do not cough over the batch.”\n\nHe sat down on the upturned crate by the annealing oven, where it is warm and where nobody working can see your face, and Wren went on reading the column.\n\n“Wren.”\n\n“Mm.”\n\n“If I tell you a thing, does it go to Master Coom?”\n\n“It depends what it is.”\n\n“That is not a no.”\n\n“It is not,” she agreed, and turned the page, and did not look at him, which is the only kindness available at that hour.\n\n“The two bushels that go up the mountain,” Bell-Anselm said. “I carry them. Sal'ka brings eleven to the gate and I take two of them up past the second bend and leave them on the flat stone, and they are gone in the morning, and Master Coom writes nine.”\n\nIdrisa Coom came down the stairs at midnight in her working coat, and Yevet came down behind her with his hat in his hands like a man at a graveside, and neither of them said anything about how long the boy had been sitting on the crate.\n\n“Show him,” Idrisa said.\n\nSo Bell-Anselm, who was twelve, stood up and took the officer of excise out to the yard gate in the dark and pointed up the ash road at nothing that anybody could see, and said: “It is four miles. It takes me an hour and a half. You can come, but you have to not talk on the last part, because it hears you.”\n\nBehind them, in the glasshouse, the fourth firing came up to colour without anyone watching it, and held there, and went on holding.","wordCount":397,"createdAt":1790321792796,"updatedAt":1790321957866},{"id":"eTdG1pkRUKTwY7KPzrURo","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"Kyy5yZWCcUl7nAeEEJtdY","text":"The ash road is not a road. It is the line where the scree stops being loose, and it climbs the shoulder of the mountain for four miles before it commits to anything.\n\nWren went up it two hours before light and caught Sal'ka at the second bend, going down, carrying nothing — which was an argument in itself, because nobody walks the ash road empty.\n\n“You did not bring the second sack,” Wren said.\n\n“I brought what I was paid to bring.”\n\nBell-Anselm counted it. He counts everything; it is the only thing he is any good at while he is coughing. Eleven bushels signed for and nine in the yard.”\n\nSal'ka stopped. Below them the village was four lamps and a red seam where the glasshouse roof had never sat true, and above them the scree went up grey into nothing at all.\n\n“Ask Idrisa,” she said.\n\n“I am asking you.”\n\n“Then you are asking the wrong end of it. I carry. She weighs. If two bushels go up the mountain instead of into the yard, it is not the carrier who wrote the number down.”\n\n“Up the mountain.”\n\n“I did not say that.”\n\n“You said it twice.”\n\nSal'ka looked at her for a while with the particular patience of a woman deciding how large a favour to do somebody else’s child. Then she stepped off the line of the road onto the loose scree, which nobody does, and went up and across at an angle, and in nine steps the grey had her.\n\nWren stood in the road until her hands hurt from the cold, and then went back down to the village, and did not tell Bell-Anselm anything at all.\n\nShe was halfway down before she understood what she had not asked. Sal'ka had come down the road carrying nothing; the sack she had set in the glasshouse two nights ago had gone up the mountain full and come back to the yard empty, and somebody had signed for eleven bushels of ash in a hand that was not Sal'ka’s and not Idrisa’s either.\n\nThe lamps in the village went out one after another as she came down to them, which is only what lamps do at that hour, and felt like something else entirely.\n\nAt the yard gate she stopped and looked back up at the scree, at the grey going up into more grey, and she said, out loud, to nobody, in the voice she used for the furnace: “What are we feeding.”\n\nNothing answered, which was the first honest thing anybody had said to her all week.","wordCount":434,"createdAt":1790321576627,"updatedAt":1790322269688},{"id":"i1XB8RMMugLzknXhISNPO","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"J392cQxe8km2uHY0b6Ljq","text":"Yevet du Marr came up the road on a hired mule at the hour when a glasshouse is least defensible: mid-morning, the fires banked, everything out on the shelves and nothing yet written in the book.\n\nHe was a small man in a good coat, with the manner of someone who has been shouted at professionally for years and found that it did not work. He set his ledger down on the cooling bench — on the cooling bench, the one surface in that room which will not take a mark — and Idrisa Coom moved it two inches onto the slate without saying anything about it at all.\n\n“Mistress Coom.”\n\n“Officer.”\n\n“You have had three firings since the quarter-day.”\n\n“We have had two and a half.”\n\n“The excise does not know what half a firing is.”\n\n“Then the excise should walk up the road and look at one,” Idrisa said, “and it would learn.”\n\nSomeone was on the stairs. Idrisa did not turn her head, and Yevet du Marr, who had been trained to notice, did not turn his either, which told her something about the kind of officer he had decided to be this morning.\n\n“There is ash going up that mountain,” Yevet said at last. “More of it than ever comes down again as glass. I am not accusing you of anything, mistress. I am telling you what the column looks like from Ferrow, where nobody alive has seen a furnace.”\n\n“And what does it look like from Ferrow?”\n\n“Like a village feeding something.”\n\nIdrisa Coom laughed once, with none of herself in it. “We are feeding something. It is eight feet high and it is under the floor you are standing on.”\n\n“And the Comber?”\n\nThe laugh stopped the way a wheel stops when a spoke goes. “Who told you that name?”\n\n“It is in the book,” he said, “twice, in your own hand, against two deliveries of ash that nobody in Ferrow can account for. I am not the excise this morning, mistress. I am a man who would rather ask you than ask the magistrate.”\n\nIdrisa Coom put her palm flat on the cooling bench, on the exact place the ledger had been, and left it there long enough to be a decision.\n\n“Come back on the fourth firing,” she said, “and I will show you what the Comber is, and then you can write in your book whatever you can bear to.”\n\nUpstairs, very quietly, a door that had been open became a door that was shut.","wordCount":424,"createdAt":1790321462370,"updatedAt":1790321501010}],"plotThreads":[],"motifs":[],"continuitySuppressions":[],"writingLogs":[{"id":"AmkIje39UFdfHxDDsv5G1","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","date":"2026-09-25","words":1638,"createdAt":1790321148655,"updatedAt":1790322355048}],"sceneRevisions":[{"id":"9T3wfEBTWsPFETx0mxj9f","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"Kyy5yZWCcUl7nAeEEJtdY","text":"The ash road is not a road. It is the line where the scree stops being loose, and it climbs the shoulder of the mountain for four miles before it commits to anything.\n\nWren went up it two hours before light and caught Sal'ka at the second bend, going down, carrying nothing — which was an argument in itself, because nobody walks the ash road empty.\n\n“You did not bring the second sack,” Wren said.\n\n“I brought what I was paid to bring.”\n\nBell-Anselm counted it. He counts everything; it is the only thing he is any good at while he is coughing. Eleven bushels signed for and nine in the yard.”\n\nSal'ka stopped. Below them the village was four lamps and a red seam where the glasshouse roof had never sat true, and above them the scree went up grey into nothing at all.\n\n“Ask Idrisa,” she said.\n\n“I am asking you.”\n\n“Then you are asking the wrong end of it. I carry. She weighs. If two bushels go up the mountain instead of into the yard, it is not the carrier who wrote the number down.”\n\n“Up the mountain.”\n\n“I did not say that.”\n\n“You said it twice.”\n\nSal'ka looked at her for a while with the particular patience of a woman deciding how large a favour to do somebody else’s child. Then she stepped off the line of the road onto the loose scree, which nobody does, and went up and across at an angle, and in nine steps the grey had her.\n\nWren stood in the road until her hands hurt from the cold, and then went back down to the village, and did not tell Bell-Anselm anything at all.","wordCount":284,"createdAt":1790321868139},{"id":"B8HuiLIkYQPcbAyrM0DxH","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"J392cQxe8km2uHY0b6Ljq","text":"@@Yevet du Marr","wordCount":3,"createdAt":1790321420476},{"id":"CUaPKLZYaiDwaeu8LkSNz","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"snRZMYe_QJp0_UVg5MKGE","text":"The furnace had been eating for two days and it was still hungry.\n\n@@Wr","wordCount":14,"createdAt":1790321157595},{"id":"NwQiwRa_-ttN_3tg_0l1w","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"Kyy5yZWCcUl7nAeEEJtdY","text":"The ash road is not a road. It is the line where the scree stops being loose, and it climbs the shoulder of the mountain for four miles before it commits to anything.\n\nWren  went up it two hours before light and caught Sal'ka at the second bend, going down, carrying nothing — which was an argument in itself, because nobody walks the ash road empty.\n\n“You did not bring the second sack,” Wren said.\n\n“I brought what I was paid to bring.”\n\n","wordCount":83,"createdAt":1790321585281},{"id":"_5Y2gLOF0saZzhVS86YGe","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"Kyy5yZWCcUl7nAeEEJtdY","text":"The ash road is not a road. It is the line where the scree stops being loose, and it climbs the shoulder of the mountain for four miles before it commits to anything.\n\nWren went up it two hours before light and caught Sal'ka at the second bend, going down, carrying nothing — which was an argument in itself, because nobody walks the ash road empty.\n\n“You did not bring the second sack,” Wren said.\n\n“I brought what I was paid to bring.”\n\nBell-Anselm counted it. He counts everything; it is the only thing he is any good at while he is coughing. Eleven bushels signed for and nine in the yard.”\n\nSal'ka stopped. Below them the village was four lamps and a red seam where the glasshouse roof had never sat true, and above them the scree went up grey into nothing at all.\n\n“Ask Idrisa,” she said.\n\n“I am asking you.”\n\n“Then you are asking the wrong end of it. I carry. She weighs. If two bushels go up the mountain instead of into the yard, it is not the carrier who wrote the number down.”\n\n“Up the mountain.”\n\n“I did not say that.”\n\n“You said it twice.”\n\nSal'ka looked at her for a while with the particular patience of a woman deciding how large a favour to do somebody else’s child. Then she stepped off the line of the road onto the loose scree, which nobody does, and went up and across at an angle, and in nine steps the grey had her.\n\nWren stood in the road until her hands hurt from the cold, and then went back down to the village, and did not tell Bell-Anselm anything at all.\n\nShe was halfway down before she understood what she had not asked. Sal'ka had come down the road carrying nothing; the sack she had set in the glasshouse two nights ago had gone up the mountain full and come back to the yard empty, and somebody had signed for eleven bushels of ash in a hand that was not Sal'ka’s and not Idrisa’s either.\n\nThe lamps in the village went out one after another as she came down to them, which is only what lamps do at that hour, and felt like something else entirely.\n\nAt the yard gate she stopped and looked back up at the scree, at the grey going up into more grey, and she said, out loud, to nobody, in the voice she used for the furnace: “What are we feeding.”\n\nNothing answered, which was the first honest thing anybody had said to her all week.","wordCount":434,"createdAt":1790322222623},{"id":"juG6x6kk7KDl1qsDZpA15","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"snRZMYe_QJp0_UVg5MKGE","text":"The furnace had been eating for two days and it was still hungry.\n\nWren Halloway crouched at the feeding door with her sleeves pushed past the old burn, watching the glory hole breathe. Orange, then white, then orange again. Master Idrisa Coom said you could read a furnace the way a midwife reads a labour — that the third firing was where a batch came right or came ruinous, and that there was no third thing. Idrisa was asleep upstairs and had been since the bell, which was how Wren knew this firing was hers.\n\nBell-Anselm came down the ladder backwards, as he always did, with the ash bucket knocking each rung.\n\n“It’s white at the back,” he said. “It’s been white at the back an hour.”\n\n“I know what it is.” Wren did not look up. “Put the bucket down and stop breathing on it.”\n\nHe put the bucket down. He was twelve and had the furnace boy’s cough already, the one Idrisa Coom said came out of the ash and not out of the cold, and Wren had stopped arguing with her about it, because arguing with Idrisa Coom was a way of losing an evening.\n\nThey watched it together. Somewhere past midnight the white went out of the back wall and the whole chamber settled to one even colour, the colour of an apricot held up against a lamp, and Wren let out a breath she had been holding since the bell.\n\n“There,” she said.\n\n“Is that it?”\n\n“That is the whole of it. Four years to learn to see that.”\n\nThe door at the yard end opened and the cold came in ahead of the woman carrying it. Sal'ka had ash on her forearms to the elbow and a sack over one shoulder. She set the sack down without being asked and looked into the glory hole the way a farrier looks at a horse she has shod before.\n\n“Third firing,” Sal'ka said. “You’re late with it.”\n\n“We’re on time with it.”\n\n“You’re late,” she said, “because I’ve come up the ash road twice this week, and the second time there was a man on it. Going up. Not coming down.”\n\nBell-Anselm looked at Wren. Wren looked at the furnace, which had nothing to say about any of it.","wordCount":383,"createdAt":1790322317515},{"id":"mLx1-VqMP54ZPLrAl0-ym","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"snRZMYe_QJp0_UVg5MKGE","text":"The furnace had been eating for two days and it was still hungry.\n\nWren Halloway crouched at the feeding door with her sleeves pushed past the old burn, watching the glory hole breathe. Orange, then white, then orange again. Master Idrisa Coom said you could read a furnace the way a midwife reads a labour — that the third firing was where a batch came right or came ruinous, and that there was no third thing. Idrisa was asleep upstairs and had been since the bell, which was how Wren knew this firing was hers.\n\nBell-Anselm ","wordCount":96,"createdAt":1790321306584},{"id":"veWEMfa_f7Qk5u2b9E3KU","worldId":"Bj1tFL0vKAXpx1Tix-MGM","eventId":"0GURVDYwffZhHY_VCAnKx","text":"The fourth firing was set for the night of the new moon, which Idrisa Coom called superstition and observed anyway.\n\n","wordCount":20,"createdAt":1790321826150}],"tombstones":[],"blobs":[]}